<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">People's Movement for the Liberation of Angola</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1956">1956</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as MPLA</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The People's Movement for the Liberation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> (MPLA) is an Angolan political party founded in the 1950s, which fought Portuguese rule from 1961 under Agostinho Neto. Backed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and Cuban troops, it took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luanda">Luanda</a> and declared Angola's independence in November 1975, then fought a long civil war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unita">UNITA</a>. It has governed Angola ever since, under José Eduardo dos Santos and João Lourenço.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mpla.jpg" alt="People's Movement for the Liberation of Angola" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: E2m · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_MPLA.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The movement grew out of two organizations: the Party of the United Struggle for Africans in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>, founded in 1953 by Viriato da Cruz and Matias Miguéis, and the Angolan Communist Party, founded in December 1955 as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luanda">Luanda</a> cell of the Portuguese Communist Party. Viriato da Cruz belonged to both and arranged their merger into a broad nationalist front. On 10 December 1956, at the house of Ilídio Machado in Luanda, Machado, da Cruz and Mário Pinto de Andrade wrote the Manifesto of 1956 calling for a broad Popular Movement for the Liberation of Angola. Machado was the first president until his arrest in 1959, and part of the leadership moved to exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> from 1957.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The MPLA took up arms against the Portuguese in 1961. In the early 1970s its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> activity declined under Portuguese counter-insurgency, and internal conflict split it for a time into three factions. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carnation-revolution">Carnation Revolution</a> of 1974 the Angolan independence coalition broke down, and on 11 November 1975 Agostinho Neto, holding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luanda">Luanda</a> and the oil fields of the Atlantic coast, declared the People's Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>. UNITA and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-of-angola">FNLA</a> declared independence separately at Huambo. South Africa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaire">Zaire</a> intervened for the FNLA and UNITA, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> intervened for the MPLA, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> aided Cuba and the MPLA.</p><p>At its first congress in 1977 the MPLA adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism-leninism">Marxism-Leninism</a> and added Partido do Trabalho to its name. After Nito Alves's attempted coup that year, Neto ordered the killing of suspected followers of orthodox <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a>; estimates of the dead range from 2,000 to 70,000, with some placing the toll at 18,000. In December 1990 the party declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-democracy">social democracy</a> its ideology. After it won the 1992 election, fighting resumed, and tens of thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unita">UNITA</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-of-angola">FNLA</a> sympathizers were killed in what is called the Halloween Massacre. The war ended in 2002 with the death of Jonas Savimbi and a ceasefire; more than 500,000 civilians died in it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The MPLA's core base is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mbundu">Ambundu</a> ethnic group and the educated intelligentsia of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luanda">Luanda</a>. Its armed wing, the People's Armed Forces for the Liberation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> (FAPLA), became the national armed forces from 1975 to 1991. Its mass organizations include the Angolan Women's Organization, the National Union of Angolan Workers, the Agostinho Neto Pioneer Organization and the Youth of the MPLA.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “MPLA” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the late 1950s the FNLA and the <b>MPLA</b> organised against Portuguese rule and forced labour.</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-angola">Portuguese Angola · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1966 the army fought the South West African People’s Organisation in the South African Border War, which spread into Angola after 1975, when South African forces intervened in the Angolan civil war against the <b>MPLA</b> government and its Cuban allies.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-south-africa">Republic of South Africa · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…parties won free elections in Lithuania in 1992, Poland in 1993 and Hungary in 1994, and several former single parties remain in power under multi-party constitutions, among them Tanzania’s Chama Cha Mapinduzi, Mozambique’s FRELIMO and Angola’s <b>MPLA</b>; scholars now study dominant-party and “electoral authoritarian” regimes alongside one-party states.</blockquote><a href="/government/one-party-state">One-party state · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
